{"step_index":126,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-28T18:23:29Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nTo build a simple yet production-grade agentic environment application, you need to assemble four fundamental building blocks rather than relying on a monolithic prompt or single API wrapper. \n\n---\n\n### **1. The Three-Layer System Architecture**\n\nEvery agentic application breaks down into three distinct, decoupled layers:\n\n* **Layer 1: Reasoning Layer (The Cognitive Core)**: The underlying LLM or local model (e.g., via Ollama/llama.cpp) that receives state, generates step-by-step thoughts, and decides which tools to invoke.\n* **Layer 2: Orchestration Layer (The Control Plane)**: The state machine or framework (e.g., LangGraph, custom Rust/Python loop) that manages thread checkpoints, short-term session state, turn limits, and error handling.\n* **Layer 3: Tool & Environment Layer**: The execution plane exposing APIs, filesystems, databases, and local terminal binaries via strict **JSON Schemas**.\n\n```\n[ User Goal ] ──► [ Orchestration Control Plane ] ──► [ Reasoning Engine ]\n                             │                              │\n                             ▼                              ▼\n                 [ Short/Long-Term Memory ]        [ Tool & Environment Layer ]\n```\n\n---\n\n### **2. Structuring the Agentic Environment: The Four Core Modules**\n\nA minimal working agentic environment application consists of four Python/Rust components:\n\n#### **A. Environment & Instruction Contract (`AGENTS.md`)**\nAt the root of your project directory, create a standardized **`AGENTS.md`** file. It serves as a machine-readable instruction contract that tells coding and execution agents your project's exact build steps, test commands, directory rules, and prohibited patterns.\n\n```markdown\n# AGENTS.md — Project Execution Contract\n## 1. Project Overview\nSimple Agentic Environment application using Python 3.12,